Date: Wed, 14 Apr 93 16:44:17 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Singularity Studies

I am looking at a graph of "10^9/Population" (inverse population
mormalized to 1 billion) plotted from 500 AD through 2000 AD.
It is a surprisingly steady trend, with only the Black Death
causing a 200 year delay in the pattern.  More recently, the
trend since 1650 AD crosses zero right about 2040 AD.

This trend has hundreds of years of history behind it, and it
says that population becomes infinite at that time.  I think
one of several things can happen.  The population can actually
become very large around that date, but most of the population
will not be DNA based.  We could defy the historical trend
by levelling out (zpg, or less).  We could break the trend
with a catastrophe of major proportions (nowadays, even killing
a billion people only delays things 11 years).

To me the fascinating part is this population trend has been
running all the way back to the dark ages, when the last big halt
occurred at the fall of the Western Roman and Chinese empires
at about the same time.

The only possibilities I see with a large chance are the ususal
nuclear war options, and the AIDS pandemic.  

Dani

