Date: Wed, 14 Apr 93 15:28:49 PDT
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: Singularity Studies

> From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)

> I am looking at a graph of "10^9/Population" (inverse population
> mormalized to 1 billion) plotted from 500 AD through 2000 AD.
> It is a surprisingly steady trend, with only the Black Death
> causing a 200 year delay in the pattern.

I'm sure you can fit all sorts of things to the data.  Choosing a
functional form which introduces a singularity seems like poor
modeling, as there is no reasonable mechanism by which to have an
infinite population.  Without mechanistic insight, you can use this
reciprocal-linear form, or a tweaked exponential, or a polynomial of
outrageous order, without any idea as to which -- if any -- will
extrapolate in a reasonable fashion.

The reason the graph you have doesn't go back before 500 A.D., BTW,
is most likely that the reciprocal-linear model will break badly if
you go far back enough in time.  I don't think it will fall off
nearly fast enough.

   Eli   ebrandt@jarthur.claremont.edu

