From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: The Singularity

Perry reposts some interesting > year-old thoughts on the "singularity".
My one-year-on-list anniversary is today, according to my archives, so
I hadn't seen it before (I wonder what the turnover rate is like...).

He postulates that the occurrence of the event will be quite dramatic
and fast, which is interesting but certainly debatable.

> Consider: once we can replace our neurons with nanomachines,
> we will be able to speed up our thought processes by at least 100
> Million times. (I personally feel the possibility of accelerating it
> by hundreds of billions of times is well within the realm of
> possibility, but lets be conservative for the moment.)

The premise:  the knowledge and technical skill necessary to successfully
replace neurons with synthetic devices will be predated by the ability
to produce sub-neuron-size machines capable of reproducing all important
neural functions at 10^8 times "bio-speed".  This may be true but it
may not -- its truth depends on a relatively mature nanotechnology
being avialable before neuron replacement; Moravec, for example,
depends on no such thing in _Mind Children_.

Rather than debate this point (which is sort of dull in 1993),
I'll just mention that a "replacement" policy of this kind (that
wants the kind of speedup Perry suggests) MUST use electronic,
rather than mechanical nanocomputers[1] and even so will necessarily
be much smaller than the wetware they replace.  My (imperfect)
memory tells me that electrical implulses in the brain travel
through axons and dendrites at about 100 meters per second (if
this is wrong, somebody please tell me because it's important).
To keep the present brain size, the signals would have to travel
at 10^10 meters per second to get Perry's 10^8 speedup and keep
the timing right.  Since the speed of light is only 3x10^8 m/sec,
the sped-up brain could at most be about 5mm across.

Not that this is necessarily a problem, mind you -- but it's
something to think about when visualizing your future.

> Consider what that means: in the time it takes you to say "hello",
> several YEARS will have passed to [the upload].

I hereby volunteer not to be first.  Could be lonely for a while!
Whether I volunteer to be second depends on who went first.

> once nanomachines capable of performing delicate
> biological work are available, the time to fall over the edge (i.e.
> the time to the end of the human race) will likely be less than a
> year, and at most two to five.

Given that this is correct, there are some interesting consequences --
perhaps most important, I damn sure want to be on the "inside" of
such an effort -- and probably so do most people on this list.
If Perry's scenario is correct, won't the vast majority of humanity
be hanging in the balance of what happens to the first few uploads?

> Regardless of what methodology you use, our capacity to predict
> anything ten years afterwards, or, more likely, two years afterwards,
> is nil. We don't know anything about what things will be like, PERIOD.

This seems to be the answer to Robin's "horizon" alternative to
the term "singularity".  When the horizon shrinks to six inches in
front of your face, it's a qualitatively different sort of phenomenon.

> try to read "Blood Music" by Greg Bear.

I second that nomination.

derek
