Date: Wed, 14 Apr 93 18:00:58 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: ECON\UPLOAD: The Singularity

Perry writes:

>Periodically, this topic comes up. This is a copy of an old (very old)
>post of mine that a couple of people told me was particularly good. 

Periodically I meet people who publicly make outrageous statements,
who on personal questioning will then backpedal and qualify to the
point of negating the main sense of their claims, but who are then
found again in public making the same outrageous statements.  Perry on
singularies seems a good example of this phenomena. 

Perry reposts:

>The definitions [of singularity], almost no matter how they are set,
>will point to approximately the same few year period in our future.
>Consider: once we can replace our neurons with nanomachines, we will
>be able to speed up our thought processes by at least 100 Million
>times. ... Once we launch our first few people over the edge with that
>sort of speedup, by the next afternoon more will have happened than
>has happened in five times our recorded history ... Several thousand
>years will be "passing" every second. Before the first DAY after
>speedup is achieved, assuming our sped up humans are equipped with
>high-speed assemblers to do their bidding, the human race will not
>merely be unrecognisable, but quite likely much of the nearby solar
>system will be unrecognisable. ... once nanomachines capable of
>performing delicate biological work are available, the time to fall
>over the edge (i.e.  the time to the end of the human race) will
>likely be less than a year, and at most two to five.

Sigh.  I guess we need to have the same conversation all over again.  
I'm pretty sure Perry will grant most of my points, but I'm less
confident this will be reflected in the next context in which he posts
on the subject.  

First, I find it highly unlikely that uploading technology will start
out at 10^8x faster on the first day.  Even if the hardware were
capable of it, you'd run them at 1x speed for a while, connected to
ordinary bodies, testing to see that you've really got the right model
of the brain in your emulation.  Initial models would likely include
lots of expensive to compute detail, perhaps with complex models of
each synapse.  It is only with much time and testing that we would
learn how to par this down to more bare essentials.

Second, to avoid going crazy, uploads would need real or simulated
bodies in some compatible world, allowing enough tactile sensations,
human contact, relaxation, sex, etc. so that uploads don't go crazy.
Sure, this would be developed eventually, but not before lots of
expensive and time consuming trial and error.

Third, running an upload 10x as fast would cost 10x as many CPUs, and
you wouldn't bother unless they could be ~10x more productive.
Otherwise you'd rather make 10x as many copies of this upload.  But to
be productive people need lots more than a dark room in which to think
alone for thousands of years.  There's only so much productivity you
could get from relatively isolated uploads, all copies of a small
number of original people, no matter how bright or fast running.  They
need to be integrated into the rest of the world economy.

Sure, maybe uploads could quickly dominate the world market for
proving math theorems, but most other economic niches require more
frequent interaction with lots of other economic actors, whose
response times are tuned for familiar interactions.  How soon can you
get focus group feedback on your new product ideas?  How long does it
take to pitch your idea to managers/funders?  How long does it take to
test product prototypes in realistic settings?  How fast can you get
new raw materials shipped?  How fast can you retool your production
line and how fast can it run?  How long to advertize and convince
customers to buy your better upload product?  How long to ship it to
them?  How long to negotiate with distributors and regulators to allow
your better product to be sold?  Hell, how long before governments
decide to allow products made from upload labor?

Once tolerated in some locale, uploads would slowly percolate out into
the economy, forming corporations and work groups to displace existing
groups.  At each stage they would have to either acquire or replace
the existing knowledge in the economy, mostly embodied in individual
people who know how to play their part within their organizational
context.  These people would have to be uploaded themselves, they
would have to allow upload replacements to watch their work habits
enough to replace them, or uploads would have to reinvent
substantially different ways to meet the same customer and
organizational needs.

And yes, eventually, uploads would come to dominate the economy.  But
*not* in one day after the first uploading was done!  To claim this is
pure hyperbole, and such hyperbole does more harm than good.  Once
skeptics can convince themselves that one prominent argument for
something is poorly grounded, they can allow themselves to dismiss the
whole category of arguments for similar-seeming conclusions.  Let's be
careful in what we claim.

Robin Hanson

