Date: Wed, 14 Apr 1993 18:28:45 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Life on the edge of k-space

Derek Zahn inquires into...
> extrapolations of current trends that can lead to a _severe_
> warping of what life will be like...

I'm hyped about a slightly different approach to forecasting
right now, thus the new subject line.  My approach 
tends towards "what-if" scenarios that, given a small 
number of assumptions about discoveries in currently 
unknown areas (eg the extent of applicability of GP, the 
possibility of smart drugs, etc.) and conservative 
assumptions about social adaptation, market demand, 
implementation effort needed, etc., give a huge payoff.  I 
acknowledge the validity of trend-following only to the 
extent it corresponds to the findings of theoretical applied 
science.  I want to peer into what Tim May calls "k-space", 
the n-dimensional space of future knowledge and 
business opportunities, and scout out the high-quality ores.

I eschew any Gantt chart with specific dates.  These often 
fail even for short-term software projects.   How about a 
PERT chart where the milestones are very well defined -- 
definitions suitable for idea futures markets.  The times & 
costs to complete the tasks are probabilistic.

One or more related tasks in the PERT can be further 
described as an exploratory business plan with market 
demand, implementation costs and  cash flow.  Risk can be 
categorized by discovery, implementation, and  market 
risks.  Market risks can further be broken out into 
regulatory,  demand and competition risk.  As an example, 
smart drugs have a high  discovery risk (who knows if 
sufficiently effective ones will be discovered), a high 
regulatory  risk (FDA does not recognize enhancement as 
a legitimate  drug category), and perhaps some demand 
risk (will they be bought & used even if they clearly 
work?)   

On the other hand the potential demand, and therefore 
cash flow is huge. If half the people on the planet can 
double their income with smart drugs,  and  they spend half 
of that increase on the smart drugs, the smart drug  market 
is  25% of the current world GNP, several $trillion per 
year!  An  investment of $100 million in such a market, on 
the high end of the cost of  bringing a drug to market, 
would give an ROI (return on investment) of c. 100,000 if 
such a project succeeded.

Risk is a variance in outcomes, which gives us the financial 
benchmark "beta". In his excellent outline on k-space, Tim 
May gives a chart showing expected  ROI as a function of 
beta and years into investment, which I will summarize 
here:

#				yrs into investment ->
#		risk/yr	beta	1	5	10
bookies		0.5	2.12	2.25	92	1e13
seed capital 	0.3	1.57	1.56	10.53	840
venture capital	0.2	1.35	1.79	4.79	123
junk bonds	0.1	1.16	1.19	2.45	10.19
govnt notes	0.01	1.02	1.07	1.44	2.40

The trend towards steep discounting of the future for high-
risk investments is clear.  Most of this is due to the cumulative 
nature of the risk (50% risk/year over 10 years gives a 1/2^10 
chance of success after 10 years), and some of it is due to the 
accumulation of interest costs.

Diversification can be used to reduce risk.  If a venture 
capital fund invests in several high-beta ventures with 
independent probabilities of failure, the combined beta of 
the portfolio is much lower. Unfortuneately, VC groups 
tend to invest in fads -- PCs  in the early 1980s, pen 
computers in the late 1980s, multimedia lately -- and this 
greatly reduces the independence of failure risk.  A single 
company can diversify regulatory and market risk by 
targetting related technology  at several markets (eg 
psychoactives research at the Alzheimer's, depression & 
smart drugs markets).

A VC fund might also greatly reduce beta  by hedging 
seed investments with futures. These instruments might  be 
idea futures that have a strong influence over the beta, eg 
a  "smart drug that can increase 50% of the population's IQ 
to 150 by 2000" discovery future.   Here the fact that IQ 
can be determined by a standard test helps  to make the 
future well-defined; questions about IQ's validity and its 
relation to market demand for smart drugs can be debated  
by the VCs when deciding how to use the future as a 
hedge.  There might be futures against regulatory action,  
if such action can be well-defined (is "FDA does not allow 
smart drugs in 2005"  sufficiently well defined?  Any 
lawyers or bookies want to tackle this?)

Futures might also be directly related to market demand 
("$10 billion smart drug sales in 2005").  In any future there 
must be sufficient interest to and low enough transaction 
costs to make a market -- this probably means the 
involvement of several VC firms and research houses in 
defining the future and investing in it.

K-space expands its territory by pushing out its knowledge 
surface, that murky area where the newly known meets 
the unkown.  Well inside of the knowledge surface are 
current businesses and well-known technologies; on the 
edges are the startups; one the fringes are new scientific 
discoveries and speculative engineering.  Peeling back the 
layers of k-space:

a: Speculative science (time travel, etc.)  [beta unkown]
b: Exploratory business plans, well-defined probabilistic milestones,          
theoretical applied science, Foresight Institute, RAND, 
grad students, etc.   [beta estimated & hedged away with futures]
c: early seed capital [beta <= 1.57]
d: venture capital [beta <= 1.35]
e: normal business ventures [beta <= 1.16]

Currently there is a big gap between (b), territory claimed 
by the statists ("long-term planning", "investment", 
"infrastructure", "spinoffs", ad  nauseum), and (c).  I'd like 
to close that gap by lowering the transaction costs of 
investing in VC mutual funds and diversifying the VC 
portfolio, and by getting a better handle on the odds of and 
ability to hedge against well-defined mileposts via idea 
futures.   We can use a probabalistic PERT chart and 
exploratory business planning to start estimating the betas 
and, when transaction costs are sufficiently low, set up idea 
