Date: Wed, 14 Apr 93 19:57:12 +0100
From: Eric Watt Forste <arkuat@joes.garage.com>
Subject: POLIT: meme warfare

As I look over the ends and means that can be called extropian, I see
that the resistance we encounter falls into two distinctly different
categories:
 
1) Opposition from dumb nature and our own ignorance. The only kind of
opposition Harry Browne thinks is worth fretting about. Overcome by
research and development of new technologies and scientific and
mathematical ideas, and by other economic activities.
 
2) Opposition by force from rude people. According to Harry Browne,
usually best evaded and defended against rather than met head on.
 
I can think of no major problems in the structure of our (planet's)
society that obstructs our work against the first sort of opposition.
No one (except the sort of radical environmentalists who are too far
out for most people to pay any attention to) seriously objects to
scientific research and economic development as such and in general.
 
But there are all sorts of memes all over the planet supporting the
second kind of opposition and obstructing our efforts to overcome it.
The reason we have governments is because most people believe that
government is a legitimate activity, and do not complain when their
employers and merchants fork over income and sales taxes on their
behalf. Some people even dutifully and honestly do complex work to
calculate their tax bills and then obediently mail in the correct
amount of valuta every quarter. I'm talking about the small but
significant minority of people who do this because they believe it is
right, not because they are afraid of the consequences of doing
otherwise.
 
The political work of extropians is the work of displacing the meme
that legitimizes governments by its memetic allele, the idea that
coercion, initiation of force, nonconsensual s/m, or whatever you'd
like to call it is generally a bad idea and ought to be resisted, even
when it takes the relatively polite form of a tax bill in your mailbox
or a business regulation to be obeyed.
 
I suspect that the desired allele is already much more widespread than
is popularly supposed; I suspect it has a high correlation with memes
that dictate not sticking your neck out, not participating in
collective activities, not putting out much effort without securing an
immediate reward, etc. This is why there are so many more libertarians
than Libertarians. If you like, you could call this set of memes the
Harry-Browne meme complex.
 
The much smaller group of libertarians have the LP meme complex:
initiation of force stinks, my life will be a lot better if people
stop initiating force against me, so I'd better identify those people
and use the rhetorical methods they know and respect to convince them
to stop. In a democracy, this group of people is everybody, and the
rhetorical methods they know and respect are called politics. So these
people organize themselves into libertarian political parties of
various sorts around the world. While I still support the LP, I have
become much more skeptical about this approach over the course of the
last year or two.
 
What have I been doing to propagate the desired allele? I've been
seeking (and finding) media that reach people who have little respect
for conventional politics and media, and exploring rhetorical
techniques effective for those people. Specifically, I've been doing
the POLITICS echo of NirvanaNET, a small local network of about seven
Bay Area BBS systems. The denizens of that echo include just about
every sort of persuasion, but Mr. and Ms. Typical Democrat and Mr. and
Ms. Typical Republican are conspicuously absent. (Probably off playing
bridge.) There are large numbers of the emphatically non-converted,
the traffic level is manageable, and the nearly exclusive use of
handles provides an environment in which people can be secure in
speaking their minds. And as an anarchist, I find it satisfying to be
able to debate in a forum in which I feel no obligation to pay lip
service to minarchist ideas.
 
The biggest problem I have encountered in presenting full-blown
voluntaryist ideas is stark incomprehension and immediate rejection.
By arguing in forums that already have a track record of thinking
seriously about whacky political ideas before rejecting them, at least
I'm already past that barrier.
 
I don't intend to suggest that the methods I'm using (at the moment)
are the right methods for anyone else. I do want to suggest that we
think of this game as being more about vectors of infection than about
traditional political discourse. And I'm very interested in hearing
about any other particularly susceptible vectors other people have
discovered, particularly any that expose people younger than 21.
 
