Date: Wed, 14 Apr 1993 23:07:04 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Life on the edge of k-space

Derek Zahn:
> Can't we multiply
> such examples endlessly[1]?  What method should we use to pick
> between such revolutionary speculations?

We stay within the bounds of theoretical applied science (cf.
_Nanosystems_ appendix A), so no flying yogis.  The "smart
drug that gives >=50% of users IQ >=150  by 2005" discovery
future is plausible via "biological analogies": there exist
people with IQ >= 150, there exist psychoactive drugs, 
there exist chemicals that effect IQ (protein and vitamins 
in malnourished children), and there exist chemicals which
improve rodent maze-solving performance.  This suggests both
plausibility and that smart drugs may be a small step in the 
n-dimensional k-space from existing products, meaning few prerequisites 
on the PERT chart. "Encompassing options" include a huge number of 
substances that can be taken orally or sublingually, can pass or
be helped across the blood-brain barrier, and have not been tested 
or used sufficiently to notice or document such an effect.  Optionally 
we might include injected drugs, implants, etc. in the future, but 
for this example I keep it simple.

Given a plausible discovery future, we estimate the market, $2 
trillion per year (see previous post), and odds of discovery and
implementation -- extremely rough guess 99% chance of failure 
for this future.  These assumptions give use a risk-adjusted $20 
billion per year market, which roughly translates to $20 billion NPV 
(net present value), making it an interesting part of k-space.

Once we've found something interesting like this, we can elaborate
further: better market analysis, a cash-flow spreadsheet, better
technical analysis (bring in the brain drug experts), etc.  At some point
we've got good enough estimates, and we've generated enough interest;
if transaction costs are low enough we start an idea futures market.  
Ideally the experts in the field come to a consensus as to demand,
costs, risks, etc. via such futures markets.

Choosing which possibilities to pursue further, exploring risks & 
markets, and defining futures are art not science, but the art is
constrained by common sense, theoretical applied science and estimates
of market and cash flow.          

> It seems like you're talking business and concrete risk,
> instead of pie-in-the-sky several-decade somebody-else-will-do-it
> projection.

Right now it's pie-in-the-sky, and most likely somebody
else will do it, but they will do it based on expected economic
return, which we can approximate with exploratory business planning:
order-of-magnitude estimates of market size, market and technological
risk, etc.  We use similar techniques as the venture capitalist
but look ahead farther and explore more risky options.  With exploratory
business planning combined with theoretical applied science we can be 
the first to find the richest ores (metaphorical ores; fertile valleys 
if your prefer :-) just beyond the current knowledge surface of k-space. 
I haven't figured out how to fend off the claims-jumpers, though,
so this is more for fun than money. :-)

The risk isn't concrete, but it isn't unknowable either; we
can use the techniques of theoretical applied science to estimate 
the chance of discovery and implementation, and normal business methods 
to estimate market size and risks. In the smart drug case, anything
between 100% and 10^-4% chance of success still gives us reason to 
look into it further, so we can be off by orders of magnitude at the 
initial stage of exploration.  If there is good reason to believe smart 
drugs _highly_ improbable, ie <10^-4%, then we skip over it and go
on to more fertile territory.  Ideally risk estimation is done by top 
researchers investing in idea futures markets; practically for now we 
get to have the fun. :-)

>[scenario: CCD for sniffing out gold]

Well, first I'd guestimate the gold prospecting market.  Gold mining
worldwide is about $10 billion per year, so gold prospecting should be
some small fraction of that.  Then, some fraction of that is instruments,
say $10 million per year.   A second way is to determine how much
new gold you can find with this device,  (perhaps not much, gold
is a well-scoured search space), and then adjust that by the risk, the 
chances that the device can be built and would actually find the gold.  
Perhaps this also comes to $10 million/year.  An NPV of $10 million 
is 2,000 times smaller than the NPV for smart drugs, so this 
seems a less interesting part of k-space, but that doesn't 
mean it couldn't be a nice niche business worthwhile for someone 
knowledgeable about gold mining, CCDs, etc. to look at (and come
up with much better estimates than mine!)

A variant on this idea is to put the CCD on a satellite around Mars, 
and look at the Mars gold mining market.  PERT prereqs include low
launch costs and/or high mass gold output/mass mining equipment,
<<$10,000/kg return transportation costs, and the discovery of
gold on Mars.  There are reasons to expect higher-quality ores
than current exist on earth, and other reasons to expect fewer
and/or lower quality ores.  Right now scientists deeply discount
such a possibility (in fact I have not seen it considered in the
planetary science literature at all).  That discount is based
on whatever preconceptions they have, not on any rational estimate
of risks, probabilities and economic payoff (that has been
published).  This PERT/business planning/theoretical applied
science kind of analysis might rectify that situation.  Furthermore, 
the idea futures market can be used to hedge the cost of exploration 
if costs are sufficiently low, based on some fraction of the gold 
mining NPV allocated to prospecting. 

Back to the Mars transport costs: if it is sufficiently promising and 
developing that transport service sufficiently easy, the mining
venture might underwrite some or all of the development costs
of the transport service.  Alternately, they might share the
costs with some other space business or wait for the service to be
developed separately.  Again, this is a task for probabilistic
PERT and exploratory business planning.

